hi welcome to biology professor today we  are talking about neuron structure  neurons are the core style of the  nervous system they are responsible for  transmitting electrical or  electrochemical signals to other cells  the most well known types of neurons are  sensory neurons and motor neurons these  are the cells that when you touch a  hotplate they transmit the signal to  your brain that Hanna talked these are  also the neurons that allow you to move  your arms around so let's look at their  structure here's an example of a neuron  here we have the nucleus in black and  the soma or cell body and you can see  that branching out of one side of the  soma are multiple dendrites these are  like fingers they allow the neuron to  reach out into its environment  and receive signals from other cells  these signals then propagate down what  is called an axon if we come over here  to look at an axon in more depth you'll  see that the axon almost looks like  beads on a string here the beads are  myelin sheaths so this is when the  compound myelin is wrapped around the  membrane of the axon and it acts as an  insulator it allows the chemical or the  electrical signal to propagate down the  axon from node to node so in between the  myelin sheaths we see the nodes of  ranvier they're also called unmyelinated  gaps and the electrical signal is being  able to hop from node to node as it goes  down the axon eventually reaching  synaptic knobs  these synaptic knobs are able to reach  out and connect with other dendrites  for example I've drawn another neuron  here but these synaptic knobs can reach  out to dendrites of multiple surrounding  neurons so that the original signal that  this neuron received gets propagated to  other nearby neurons and that's  basically how the nervous system makes  things happen in your body
 

